/// The consumer declares its own `String` host type and imports the
/// dependency's `dep_take`, which consumes the dependency's *own*
/// `String`. Identity-exact nominal typing
/// (`specs/language.md` § Nominal types) keeps the two `String`s distinct:
/// `String` here is `(main, String)`, the dependency's is
/// `(bdep/text, String)`.
///
/// The argument is a string literal *annotated* with the consumer's own
/// `String` — `"hi"(String)`. A literal's annotation resolves it to that
/// host type's identity-exact `(module, name)`, here `(main, String)`,
/// so the literal is the consumer's `String`. `dep_take`'s parameter
/// (qualified in its *declaring* module) wants
/// `(bdep/text, String)`. The annotated literal is therefore a type
/// error in the LITERAL-annotation slot — exit 14.
///
/// The annotation is load-bearing: a *bare* `"hi"` would resolve via
/// tier 2 to the expected `(bdep/text, String)` and be accepted; the
/// `(String)` annotation forces the consumer's `(main, String)` and
/// exposes the distinctness.
module main;

import bdep/text(dep_take);

host type String role(str);

pub fn mix() -> String { dep_take("hi"(String)) }
